#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "011cf746-baa5-5395-8515-3beb98ff22a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5302" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4.$ Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b695010-193a-5cbf-8437-deaea37d1e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439445} {:answer/id #uuid "c3eb42b0-64fa-57df-989d-34ad71b77544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b695010-193a-5cbf-8437-deaea37d1e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439445} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05fb1c2-71ab-5897-b477-b35b2b52f361" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07352cca-ef41-500b-abb4-1723978e1d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439447} {:answer/id #uuid "b86e32e1-d38b-5979-9b96-72ba9376f584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07352cca-ef41-500b-abb4-1723978e1d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bab6831-3422-5ec6-825e-7496bd51fcff" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a883da7c-a136-5cbf-9a7a-a47b5396a663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439449} {:answer/id #uuid "ef060659-9b12-59fd-894d-bae2fed93853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a883da7c-a136-5cbf-9a7a-a47b5396a663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439449} :answer-field/id #uuid "a280265d-1249-5f5f-8a87-e044b6bc33ae" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ce65c6-c9ad-50c2-b008-fca69d6b6025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439451} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3a6b46-9a94-5ba3-bf13-cf894946a7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ce65c6-c9ad-50c2-b008-fca69d6b6025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439451} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a35fdf6-4ccf-52f4-a7be-125347104920" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=- \\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $x=0$\n- **IV.** $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523299} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d48d2da-f7ad-5f3f-ad75-b0e9b627f848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523305} :answer-field/id #uuid "92131e32-9ef2-589e-b0eb-e5ab25c20d08" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-278259" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x+1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7b6d67e-403e-5532-986e-300f36aad2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439689} {:answer/id #uuid "91942f1d-606f-5b63-a425-9df4530badcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91942f1d-606f-5b63-a425-9df4530badcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439690} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b788bf-cfb8-5ba6-8cc4-ef42c59162fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cda3a068-cf24-5df6-b220-85d36c88acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439691} {:answer/id #uuid "9c304104-fa26-568e-a4e1-871dce80a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cda3a068-cf24-5df6-b220-85d36c88acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439691} :answer-field/id #uuid "28afb6fc-4a82-57ee-823e-e1e7c254b635" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfffc5a-1c51-522c-989e-2b35810963b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439693} {:answer/id #uuid "7fab1ccc-d963-5459-9020-11b73c257672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfffc5a-1c51-522c-989e-2b35810963b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439693} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d597da-262e-5086-a470-22ba14674ce1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440204} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7475000b-3919-52bf-8008-6c95380eee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439695} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c419fa-23be-5022-afd3-dc3877e8a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3c419fa-23be-5022-afd3-dc3877e8a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439696} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a79d45-b0d7-5b88-a614-9498abaf6c1d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440205}] :question/id #uuid "9c5d95ad-bb5b-5734-95d6-fffbd7781121" :question/math-academy-id "q-39796" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2}{2 - x} + \\frac{3}{2 + x}$.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=1$\n- **IV.** $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "After cancellation, $f(x)=\\frac{x - 10}{x^{2} - 4}$. Its real denominator zeros are $\\left\\{-2, 2\\right\\}$; canceled factors produce holes, not vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02268068-5914-5785-8518-e18bf53b47ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523253} {:answer/id #uuid "6e47da48-817d-5b8a-a761-63f096255e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523480} {:answer/id #uuid "7894bf30-9f2a-5795-9fd3-911c5345c7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523500} {:answer/id #uuid "a21c5c14-b3d6-5991-86ee-53ead623f9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523593} {:answer/id #uuid "fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523783} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc072fa3-a3a2-5452-b980-3ee1ebbabe24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c29f5a25-a27e-5aa7-83e3-1b45bff33614" :question/math-academy-id "q-4698" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(8{x}^{3}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x[(2x)^{3}+{1}^{3}]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the sum of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- Solving $2x+1=0$ gives the solution $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-2x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =4-16 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec569be-ba0a-519b-9bc2-090d3a2f231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523286} {:answer/id #uuid "2888a284-8331-5647-a1fb-6327fa60225e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=-1,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523337} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec6d5e0-dc19-53cc-b6c1-165c91fc49be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8,\\,x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523521} {:answer/id #uuid "ab84236c-d242-5f31-bc2e-1fab3d6a3a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523616} {:answer/id #uuid "c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523662} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb45bf1-efa9-5849-a776-e098b44b30ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "350d70bb-36ec-51d9-b44c-9369bfdb5b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-6929" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2x+1)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x+2)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions  $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05946333-97e1-583b-b079-89c0440789ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523265} {:answer/id #uuid "64e6a2ce-1827-54b2-abce-9bceebd0f865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523461} {:answer/id #uuid "7077a196-2c6e-5544-b0a6-fb1a04fcea93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523486} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff55830-2c39-53c2-b7e6-cfbcb904ef14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523556} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523770} :answer-field/id #uuid "287da6b6-7396-5875-9477-af19bd2b3d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70552" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523329} {:answer/id #uuid "6326b577-cdc2-5a37-9ed8-b6dd08c069ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523457} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff2983b-2294-55ca-b354-69dd5a064fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523525} {:answer/id #uuid "eb772194-8f56-57ad-86a4-b559796a524b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523746} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbd1c4d-40aa-55ef-a2ce-01968b4f8725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523329} :answer-field/id #uuid "da797173-8fb0-5fa8-932c-55153821e90c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80681" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}-x+2=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =1-8 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a23df9c-0bb4-5f06-a2ce-f50dbe498d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439993} {:answer/id #uuid "15b3d22f-4cec-5498-90ba-670c1d780658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a23df9c-0bb4-5f06-a2ce-f50dbe498d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439993} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9ab1ab-d571-5366-9440-7a3c113c7d35" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e50e6fe-de04-5702-9faf-549a10b505fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439995} {:answer/id #uuid "fe516876-d9e1-54d1-84bc-ee6388f07ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e50e6fe-de04-5702-9faf-549a10b505fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439995} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ddbc92-6bd1-5484-8f57-506f318d7cee" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "370a76e0-2c04-500b-a841-77d0ae40552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439997} {:answer/id #uuid "91966088-bf02-5175-b84e-d585c7568317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "370a76e0-2c04-500b-a841-77d0ae40552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439997} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e0421e-636c-51a4-83fb-f74c8f1eae44" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}]}
